1. The Call Stack (The "Bucket Brigade")
Think of the flow of a request like a chain of command.

A request hits your Controller (getEmpByIdController).

The Controller calls the Service (getEmpByIdService).

The Service calls the Repository (findByIdRepo).

If the Repository or Service throws an EmployeeNotFoundException, it doesn't just magically teleport to the GlobalExceptionHandler. It propagates up the call stack.

The Service throws it.

The Service method stops executing.

The exception is passed back to the Controller method that called it.

Because the Controller method doesn't have a try-catch block, the Controller method stops executing.

The exception is passed back to Spring's main front controller (DispatcherServlet).

2. Spring's DispatcherServlet catches it
When Spring's DispatcherServlet receives an exception from the controller, it essentially says:
"Okay, the controller failed. Did the developer write a specific @ExceptionHandler for this exception anywhere?"

It then searches all classes annotated with @RestControllerAdvice looking for a matching @ExceptionHandler(EmployeeNotFoundException.class). If it finds one, it runs that method and sends the result back to the client.